Your project targets **net10.0**, so the best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with full coverage.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # in your test project
```

**2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`):**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
// Pre-create directories as needed
mockFs.Directory.CreateDirectory("/output");

var service = new MyService(mockFs);
Assert.True(service.DirectoryExists("/output"));
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Read("/data/config.json"));
```

This avoids writing custom wrappers — `IFileSystem` already maps 1:1 to `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, etc. Just replace `File.ReadAllText(...)` → `fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(...)` at each call site.
